R=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1];physics=json.loads((R/'reports/physics-results.json').read_text());vision=json.loads((R/'reports/vision/vision-results.json').read_text());p=json.loads((R/'Content/RobotData/robot-parameters.json').read_text())
e=html.escape
rows=[]
for x in physics['results']:
 metrics=' · '.join(k.replace('_',' ')+': '+(f'{v:.4f}' if isinstance(v,float) else str(v)) for k,v in x.items() if k not in ['test','passed'])
 rows.append(f'<tr><td>{e(x["test"])}</td><td class="{ "pass" if x["passed"] else "fail"}">{"PASS" if x["passed"] else "NEEDS WORK"}</td><td>{e(metrics)}</td></tr>')
passed=sum(x['passed'] for x in physics['results']);total=len(rows)
s='''<!doctype html><html lang="en"><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>Home robot · Unreal simulation</title><style>
*{box-sizing:border-box}body{margin:0;background:#f3f5f1;color:#243333;font:16px/1.65 system-ui,sans-serif}main{max-width:1120px;margin:auto;padding:48px 26px}h1{font-size:clamp(34px,5vw,60px);line-height:1.06;letter-spacing:-2px;margin:10px 0 20px}h2{font-size:28px;margin-top:48px}h3{margin-top:0}p{max-width:850px}.eyebrow{font-size:12px;letter-spacing:2px;color:#4e7770;text-transform:uppercase}.lead{font-size:20px}.cards{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr);gap:16px;margin:28px 0}.card{border:1px solid #d4dfd8;background:white;border-radius:12px;padding:22px}.card strong{display:block;font-size:28px}.note{border-left:4px solid #b68534;background:#fff5dd;padding:20px;border-radius:4px}.gallery{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:16px}img{width:100%;border-radius:8px;display:block}figure{margin:0}figcaption{font-size:13px;color:#5b6e69;margin-top:8px}a{color:#216b64}table{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:14px;background:white}td,th{text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #dce3df;padding:12px;vertical-align:top}th{font-size:12px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5a7069}.pass{color:#176545;font-weight:700;white-space:nowrap}.fail{color:#99480c;font-weight:700;white-space:nowrap}pre{padding:20px;background:#223531;color:#d7e8df;overflow:auto;font-size:13px;border-radius:8px}code{font-size:13px}.scroll{overflow:auto}.small{font-size:13px;color:#63756e}button{border:1px solid #9bb8af;background:white;color:#24483d;padding:10px 18px;border-radius:7px;cursor:pointer}details{margin:20px 0}summary{cursor:pointer;font-weight:600}nav{display:flex;gap:20px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin:24px 0} @media(max-width:700px){.cards,.gallery{grid-template-columns:1fr}main{padding:28px 18px}}
</style><main><div class="eyebrow">Home robot R1 / Unreal Engine 5.8.1 / 3 October 2026</div><h1>Test the robot before<br>printing the robot.</h1><p class="lead">A working local simulation on your Mac: physical wheels and rear caster, a furnished five-room apartment, stereo cameras and a separate software controller.</p><nav><a href="http://127.0.0.1:8771/autonomy.html">Robot controls</a><a href="#autonomy">Autonomous modes</a><a href="#house">House preview</a><a href="#results">Test results</a><a href="#vision">Camera mapping</a><a href="#run">Run it</a><a href="#limits">What remains</a></nav>
<h2 id="house">Explore the tiled house</h2><p>Living room, kitchen and dining area, bedroom, study and bathroom. Traditional 30 × 30 cm terrazzo tiles use irregular stone chips in pale cement and narrow joints. All walls and the ceiling have warm off-white paint with very subtle uneven tone and grain. The clean floor has a mild polished finish. These are approximate materials, not measurements of your home; no tracking markers were added. The ceiling is visible to the robot cameras and hidden only in the overhead inspection view.</p><iframe title="Interactive furnished house" src="house-preview.html" style="width:100%;height:730px;border:1px solid #c8d5cc;border-radius:12px" loading="lazy"></iframe><p><a href="house-preview.html">Open the larger interactive preview ↗</a> · Drag to rotate, scroll to zoom, Shift-drag to pan. Use the view buttons for a floor plan or robot-eye view. Browser rendering is a static inspection of the same geometry; actual physics and cameras run in Unreal.</p><div class="note">The latest autonomous test uses stereo images, encoders and a gyro. It chooses exploration targets itself and can load saved landmarks before navigating. Full-house coverage and reliable performance on real floors are not established. See the measured results below.</div><div class="cards"><div class="card"><strong>@@TESTS@@</strong>physics checks passed</div><div class="card"><strong>@@FRAMES@@</strong>pairs tracked in the earlier plain-tile test</div><div class="card"><strong>@@MASS@@ kg</strong>estimated robot mass</div></div>
<div class="note"><strong>The 10 mm threshold is a design risk.</strong> @@THRESHOLD@@ The longer house route (box robot) accumulated a large return-position error. Treat this as a result that still needs repeatability and contact-model validation. Compare with a measured caster and motor model before relying on the result for a hardware build.</div>
<figure><img src="reports/unreal-running.png"><figcaption>The robot running in Unreal on this computer.</figcaption></figure>@@AUTONOMY@@<h2 id="results">Baseline physics checks</h2><p>These earlier isolated fixtures launch Unreal and exercise Chaos rigid-body physics. They do not establish full-house navigation; the core physics controller is unchanged, while the house and preview have been updated. The round chassis turns about its centre and is free to pitch and roll. @@NOCASTER@@ Keeping the single rear caster in place supports the chassis. Commands come from a separate Python process, while wheel control runs in a portable C++ core.</p><div class="scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Test</th><th>Result</th><th>Measured in simulation</th></tr></thead><tbody>@@ROWS@@</tbody></table></div><p class="small">One run per listed case. Controller logic also passed standalone checks for startup, acceleration and torque limits, watchdog, faults, re-arming and invalid commands. <a href="reports/physics-results.json">Machine-readable results</a> · <a href="reports/physics-traces.json">Recorded telemetry</a></p>
<h2 id="vision">Earlier plain-tile experiment</h2><p>This historical recording used a predefined encoder route and plain 60 cm tiles, before the autonomous modes, terrazzo, white ceiling and surface reflections were added.</p><div class="note">@@HOUSE_RESULT@@</div><p>The virtual stereo pair has a 62 mm baseline at a height of 150 mm. It produces paired 640 × 360 images with a shared simulation timestamp. The tiled house has plain walls, repeated grout lines, furniture legs and occlusion. This is a substantially harder scene than the earlier calibration room.</p><div class="gallery"><figure><img src="reports/vision/stereo-left.png"><figcaption>Earlier plain-tile test: left camera.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="reports/vision/stereo-right.png"><figcaption>Right camera: same instant, shifted by the stereo baseline.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="reports/vision/stereo-disparity.png"><figcaption>Computed stereo disparity. Black means no accepted depth; warmer colors generally indicate nearer surfaces.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="reports/vision/trajectory.png"><figcaption>Camera-only motion estimate versus simulator position, which is used only for scoring.</figcaption></figure></div><p><strong>@@VISION@@</strong></p>@@LOSS@@<details><summary>Earlier easy-scene baseline</summary><p>The earlier patterned calibration room tracked 66 of 66 pairs on a 40 cm square, with about 2 cm maximum position error. That result only established that the pipeline ran; it does not establish reliable tracking in a normal home. The original recording and <a href="reports/vision-calibration/vision-results.json">measurements</a> are preserved separately.</p></details><p>The vision program matches features between images, obtains their depth from the stereo pair, and estimates camera motion with RANSAC/PnP. Accepted depth points are accumulated into a colored 3D point cloud. This historical cloud is sparse and contains stereo outliers. The newer autonomous controller builds a separate occupancy map from filtered stereo rays. It stops and reports a loss if tracking fails; it does not substitute the simulator position.</p><p>The newer controller adds saved-landmark relocalization, occupancy mapping, frontier selection, path planning and live obstacle checks. Global pose-graph optimization, reliable full-house exploration and hardware integration remain unfinished.</p><figure><img src="reports/vision/map-top-view.png"><figcaption>Top view of the local point cloud, with floor points filtered out.</figcaption></figure><p><a href="reports/vision/stereo-map.ply">Download the colored 3D point cloud</a> · <a href="reports/vision/vision-results.json">Vision measurements</a></p>
<h2 id="run">Run the robot</h2><ol><li>Double-click <strong>Scripts/launch.command</strong> in the delivered folder. It opens the house in Unreal.</li><li>Double-click <strong>Scripts/start-autonomy.command</strong>. On first use it installs the vision libraries into a private environment.</li><li>Open <a href="http://127.0.0.1:8771/autonomy.html">Robot controls</a> and choose <strong>Start Mapping</strong>. The robot scans, chooses reachable frontiers and saves its progress.</li><li>Select a pale free-space point on the robot’s map, then choose <strong>Go to selected position</strong>. The robot first recognizes saved landmarks, plans a path, checks its position while moving and returns to Idle after confirming arrival.</li><li><strong>Idle / Stop</strong> stops motion and saves partial mapping. Failure to locate itself, a blocked route or a sensor stop is reported without claiming arrival.</li><li>In Unreal, use <strong>1</strong> for follow, <strong>2</strong> for robot eye, <strong>3</strong> for house orbit and <strong>4</strong> for top. Drag or use arrows to rotate, and scroll or use + / − to zoom. These controls do not move the stereo sensors.</li></ol><p>Run only one simulation and one controller at a time. The autonomous controller owns the motor switch while running. For manual driving, stop that controller first; then use P for power, R to arm, W/S and A/D to move, Space for motor power OFF and C to record stereo. The older drive-house launcher follows a predefined diagnostic route and is separate from the autonomous modes.</p><p>Maps are stored under <code>Saved/Maps</code>; recordings are stored in individual <code>Saved/Stereo</code> folders. Ground truth is retained only for independent scoring and is discarded by the robot's sensor adapter. The package includes selected evaluation recordings and saved maps; longer development recordings remain in the local project.</p>
<details><summary>Build, repeat tests and process camera recordings</summary><p>The source project opens in Unreal Editor via <code>HomeRobotSim.uproject</code>. The scene is created when Play starts, so the editor's empty entry map is expected. The double-click launcher opens the game directly.</p><pre>./Scripts/build.command
# With the graphical game closed:
python3 tests/run_physics.py
# With the house game running:
python3 controller/drive_demo.py --side .4 --capture
# Camera processing (requires Python 3.10+):
python3 -m venv .venv
.venv/bin/pip install -r controller/requirements.txt
.venv/bin/python controller/stereo_mapping.py</pre><p>Choose another scene with <code>./Scripts/launch.command obstacle</code>, <code>cliff</code>, <code>threshold</code> or <code>empty</code>. Set <code>UE_ROOT</code> if Unreal is installed elsewhere. macOS build tools and Xcode 26.1.1 were present and used successfully.</p></details>
<details><summary>Software interface and project structure</summary><pre>Source/HomeRobotSim/DriveCore.h     wheel PI control and stop state machine
Source/HomeRobotSim/RobotPawn.cpp  physics, sensors, stereo and UDP
Source/HomeRobotSim/HouseScene.cpp shared furnished-house loader
Scripts/build_house.py             editable room and furniture geometry
Content/RobotData/                 CAD visuals and estimated masses
controller/robot_client.py         hardware-independent sensor/command boundary
controller/drive_demo.py           encoder-based square route
controller/stereo_mapping.py       earlier offline visual odometry
controller/autonomy_service.py     live Mapping / Go to position controller
controller/autonomy/               stereo, localization, occupancy and planning
tests/                            controller and real Unreal regression tests
reports/                          results and generated vision outputs</pre><p>Command example: <code>{"v_m_s":0.1,"w_rad_s":0,"arm":true}</code>. Send velocity heartbeats at 20 Hz; loss for 0.35 seconds disarms the controller. Switch fields are <code>motor_power_off</code> and <code>motor_power_on</code>. Turning power on does not arm. Legacy <code>estop</code>/<code>release_estop</code> input aliases are retained.</p><p>Robot data use metres, seconds, +X forward, +Y left, +Z up. Unreal internally uses centimetres and a reflected Y axis. Stereo camera coordinates are X right, Y down, Z forward. The controller can later use a serial Pico adapter and real camera frames; no Pico firmware has been flashed or proven on hardware.</p></details>
<h2 id="limits">What this simulation can establish</h2><p>It checks that the code runs, sensor messages have consistent units, the controller reacts to specified faults, and a vision pipeline can estimate movement from a controlled synthetic scene. It cannot establish real motor torque, power integrity, battery condition, camera calibration or floor-sensor reliability.</p><ul><li>Masses are estimates from CAD and component allowances; chassis inertia is approximated by a box and shifted center of mass.</li><li>Wheel torque is capped at an assumed 0.10 N·m per motor. Gearmotor reflected inertia is approximated as 0.0005 kg·m² on each wheel axle. Friction and drag are assumed.</li><li>The caster is an ideal sphere with a bearing constraint. Threshold results are particularly sensitive to contact physics, available torque, weight and caster geometry.</li><li>Stereo uses an ideal pinhole camera, synthetic 80° horizontal field of view and zero lens distortion. Real rolling shutter, exposure mismatch, USB latency and calibration error are not yet modeled.</li><li>Encoder counts are quantized, but sensor noise, transparent/black floors, stale range readings, dropouts and electronics faults still need dedicated cases.</li><li>The 8 × 6 m furnished sample house is a test fixture; it is not a scan of your home. The earlier encoder route visited three rooms. Current autonomous coverage is partial and does not validate every room. The current scene has approximate screen-space floor reflections; off-screen reflected objects, transparent glass, true mirrors and real sensor noise are not represented. Rounded household props have conservative box collision envelopes.</li></ul><p>The updated physical design uses a normal motor ON/OFF toggle and organized cable bundles. Wire envelopes clear all three chassis plates in CAD, and the 27 print parts fit the Prusa MK3S bed. <a href="robot/design-review.html#model">Open the revised assembly</a>.</p><p class="small">References: <a href="https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/en-us/unreal-engine/macos-development-requirements-for-unreal-engine">Epic's macOS requirements</a> and <a href="https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/en-us/unreal-engine/API/Runtime/Engine/USceneCaptureComponent2D">Unreal scene capture API</a>. The project is a prototype testbed, not a completed autonomous robot stack.</p></main></html>'''

autonomy_path=R/'reports/autonomy-validation.json'
autonomy=json.loads(autonomy_path.read_text()) if autonomy_path.exists() else {}
checks=autonomy.get('summary','Live validation is still in progress. Do not treat this as a full-house success.')
autonomy_section=f'''<h2 id="autonomy">The robot controls movement</h2><p><strong>Mapping</strong> rotates to observe the room, triangulates nearby points from both cameras, builds free space and obstacles, then selects its own reachable unexplored boundary. It revisits uncertain areas and saves partial progress. A heuristic stopping rule applies only to observed reachable space; it cannot certify unseen rooms.</p><p><strong>Go to position</strong> recognizes saved 3D landmarks before translating, plans through known free space and corrects its motion from camera observations. It stops travel and searches again if confidence drops. Arrival requires five camera updates within 10 cm of the target estimate, then it switches to Idle.</p><div class="note">{e(checks)} <a href="reports/autonomy-validation.json">Detailed autonomous results</a>.</div><div class="gallery"><figure><img src="reports/autonomy-dashboard.png"><figcaption>The robot-built map and actual left camera. Gray space is unknown.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="reports/surfaces/polished-left.png"><figcaption>Current Unreal camera: white painted walls, traditional terrazzo and lightly polished finish.</figcaption></figure></div><h3 style="margin-top:26px">Reflections and uncertain matches</h3><p>Depth is accepted only when the two stereo directions agree and the estimated depth error is small enough. Features pass geometric outlier checks; occupancy updates reject reconstructed points below the floor. Temporal tracking and saved landmarks provide additional checks. These safeguards do not guarantee rejection of every reflection.</p><p>The polished and matte captures were compared at the same stationary pose. The finish changes the rendered camera pixels, but this is a material check, not proof of robust navigation across all clean floors. Screen-space reflections omit objects outside the camera view. <a href="reports/surfaces/validation.json">Material comparison</a>.</p><h3>Using the Jetson</h3><p>The default ORB tracker is lightweight. An optional SuperPoint + LightGlue backend is included for experiments. In a five-frame desktop CPU comparison, learned matching produced more temporal matches on the difficult frame but cost about 0.8–0.9 seconds per pair, versus about 0.02–0.03 seconds for ORB after warm-up. More matches alone do not prove better localization.</p><p>The Jetson has not been benchmarked. Original Nano and Orin boards need different compatible software environments. The current desktop PyTorch installation is not a validated Nano deployment. Benchmark the actual board before selecting a learned model; the live controller stops motion when camera processing falls behind.</p><details><summary>Optional learned-feature experiment</summary><p>Use a separate Python 3.12 environment on a desktop. Install a supported PyTorch/torchvision build, then the normal controller requirements and <code>controller/requirements-learned.txt</code>. Run <code>controller/autonomy_service.py --backend superpoint_lightglue</code>. Rebuild the map when changing feature backends. For Jetson, choose a PyTorch build compatible with its JetPack release first. No automatic Jetson installer is supplied.</p><p><a href="https://github.com/cvg/LightGlue">Official LightGlue implementation</a> · <a href="https://docs.nvidia.com/deeplearning/frameworks/install-pytorch-jetson-platform-release-notes/pytorch-jetson-rel.html">NVIDIA Jetson compatibility</a> · <a href="reports/feature-benchmark-orb.json">ORB timing</a> · <a href="reports/feature-benchmark-superpoint_lightglue.json">Learned timing</a></p></details><p class="small">Prototype limits: confidence is heuristic; there is no global pose-graph optimization, no dynamic-person avoidance validation, and no real camera/Jetson/motor integration yet. Mapping and navigation use conservative 19 cm clearance and at most 7.5 cm/s.</p>'''
s=s.replace('@@AUTONOMY@@',autonomy_section)

metrics=vision
vt=f'{metrics["frames_tracked"]} of {metrics["frames_available"]} pairs were tracked.'
if metrics['tracking_complete']:vt+=' Tracking completed this recording.'
else:vt+=' Tracking was lost at frame '+str(metrics['records'][-1]['frame'])+'. The camera-only mapper is not yet reliable on this tiled-house route.'
if metrics['max_error_m'] is not None:vt+=f' Maximum position error before any tracking loss: {metrics["max_error_m"]*100:.1f} cm; this does not score the untracked remainder.'
route_path=R/'reports/house-route.json'
route=json.loads(route_path.read_text()) if route_path.exists() else {}
hr=('The encoder controller reached all its estimated waypoints. ' if route.get('controller_finished') else 'The controller stopped before finishing. ')
if not route.get('physical_route_passed'):hr+=f'<strong>The physical route failed:</strong> final target error was {route.get("final_goal_error_m",0)*100:.0f} cm (15 cm test tolerance). '
else:hr+='The physical route met the waypoint tolerance. '
hr+=('The image-only mapper tracked the recording.' if metrics['tracking_complete'] else '<strong>The image-only mapper lost tracking on the plain tiled scene.</strong> The encoder controller can continue even when the offline camera estimate fails; these are separate results.')
hr+=' <a href="reports/house-route.json">Route measurements</a>.'
loss='<figure><img src="reports/vision/tracking-loss.png"><figcaption>Actual left-camera image at the first tracking failure. The software stops the map here rather than filling in a pose from the simulator.</figcaption></figure>' if not metrics['tracking_complete'] else ''
s=s.replace('@@HOUSE_RESULT@@',hr).replace('@@LOSS@@',loss)
# Narrative numbers come from the current physics results (round robot), not from earlier runs.
by={x['test']:x for x in physics['results']}
nc=next((x for n,x in by.items() if n.startswith('negative control: remove rear caster')),None)
nocaster=(f'Removing the rear caster makes it tip back by {nc["peak_nose_up_pitch_deg"]:.1f}° (rear shell rim contact at {nc["expected_rim_contact_pitch_deg"]:.1f}°).'
          if nc and 'peak_nose_up_pitch_deg' in nc else 'The no-caster negative control has not been run for the round robot.')
th=[x for n,x in by.items() if ('threshold' in n or 'rug' in n) and 'min_shell_floor_clearance_cm' in x]
threshold=(f'{sum(x["passed"] for x in th)} of {len(th)} threshold/rug rows passed; the lowest front-skin clearance was {min(x["min_shell_floor_clearance_cm"] for x in th):.2f} cm '
           f'(the caster can stand on about {th[0].get("caster_step_limit_mm",float("nan")):.1f} mm before the front skin grounds).' if th else 'The round robot has not yet been run over the threshold fixtures.')
s=s.replace('@@NOCASTER@@',e(nocaster)).replace('@@THRESHOLD@@',e(threshold))
for k,v in {'TESTS':f'{passed} / {total}','FRAMES':f'{metrics["frames_tracked"]} / {metrics["frames_available"]}','MASS':f'{p["total_mass_kg"]:.2f}','ROWS':''.join(rows),'VISION':vt}.items():s=s.replace('@@'+k+'@@',v)
(R/'simulation-review.html').write_text(s)
